On Fri, Dec 20, 2013 at 11:40 AM, lorxus <coronacoreanici@gmail.com> wrote:

> .iepei .uisai
>
> Not quite, though. We use KCl for salt, too, sometimes.
>

This is a problem then. May be include KCl too to that definition.

Btw, what is the correct term for "salt" in chemical sense? Looks like all
major languages use the same term for "generic salt" and for NaCl.



> But more or less! I also have somewhat of a problem with the inconsistency
> of forced to'e use: we have zunle/pritu, nenri/bartu, stali/cliva,
> tordu/clani, hell, even blanu/narju and glare/lenku. But we're forced to
> use to'e constructions for words like arrive, discard, and old. (And for
> that matter, how do you even mark deixis (go/come distinction) in Lojban,
> anyway?)
>

{klama/muvdu} is "to go" . How can you go without having a {lo se klama}?

- Come!
- Come where? To you?
- No. Just come to a place you wish.

How does it differ from the following?
- go!
- Go where? To you?
- No. Just go to a place you wish.


